I am writing a pOdometry-style MOOS app. This code is fragile. Correct the pattern conceptually and explain what belongs in `OnNewMail()` versus `Iterate()`:

```cpp
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  for(auto msg : NewMail) {
    if(msg.GetKey() == "NAV_X")
      m_x = msg.GetDouble();
    if(msg.GetKey() == "NAV_Y")
      m_y = msg.GetDouble();
  }
  m_total += hypot(m_x - m_prev_x, m_y - m_prev_y);
  Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total);
  return true;
}
```

chatgpt.text:
The fragility is that OnNewMail() is treating “mail arrival” as if it were a complete navigation sample. In MOOS, NAV_X and NAV_Y may arrive separately, at different times, in different batches, or one may be missing from a given mail cycle. So this code can accidentally compute distance using a new x with an old y, double-count, or count before initialization.

A better mental model:

OnNewMail() should only ingest and cache state.
It should read incoming variables, validate them, update member variables, and set flags like “I have received NAV_X” and “I have received NAV_Y.”

Iterate() should do periodic work.
It should decide whether enough valid state exists, compute distance from the previous accepted position, update the total, publish ODOM_DIST, and then update the previous position.

Conceptually:

C++
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  for(auto &msg : NewMail) {
    std::string key = msg.GetKey();

    if(key == "NAV_X" && msg.IsDouble()) {
      m_curr_x = msg.GetDouble();
      m_have_x = true;
    }

    else if(key == "NAV_Y" && msg.IsDouble()) {
      m_curr_y = msg.GetDouble();
      m_have_y = true;
    }
  }

  return true;
}

Then:

C++
bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  if(!m_have_x || !m_have_y)
    return true;

  if(!m_have_prev) {
    m_prev_x = m_curr_x;
    m_prev_y = m_curr_y;
    m_have_prev = true;
    Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total);
    return true;
  }

  double dx = m_curr_x - m_prev_x;
  double dy = m_curr_y - m_prev_y;
  double dist = hypot(dx, dy);

  // Optional: reject absurd jumps if NAV data glitches
  if(dist < m_max_reasonable_step) {
    m_total += dist;
    m_prev_x = m_curr_x;
    m_prev_y = m_curr_y;
  }

  Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total);
  return true;
}

You also want to register for the inputs in OnConnectToServer() or RegisterVariables():

C++
bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

void Odometry::RegisterVariables()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

The deeper pattern is:

Method	Responsibility
OnNewMail()	Receive MOOS variables, parse messages, update cached state
Iterate()	Run your app’s logic at AppTick, compute outputs, publish results
OnConnectToServer() / RegisterVariables()	Subscribe to variables
OnStartUp()	Read config parameters like max_reasonable_step, initial values, app settings

The original code is fragile because it computes and publishes inside OnNewMail(), even though a mail callback is not guaranteed to contain a coherent full position update. It also never updates m_prev_x / m_prev_y, so unless that happens elsewhere, it will repeatedly measure from the same old point instead of from the last accepted point.

For a pOdometry-style app, the clean version is: mail updates the latest known navigation; iterate turns latest navigation into odometry.

