The core bug here is that distance accumulation and publishing are happening in OnNewMail(), which is the wrong place.

The core bug here is that distance accumulation and publishing are happening in OnNewMail(), which is the wrong place. Let me break down why, and what the correct pattern looks like.

Why This Is Fragile

OnNewMail() is called per mail batch, not on a stable tick. The problems:

Partial updates trigger computation. If NAV_X arrives in one mail call and NAV_Y arrives in the next, you compute distance with a stale Y (or stale X). You're diffing against m_prev_x/y before both coordinates are updated.
m_prev_x / m_prev_y are never updated. So m_total grows forever — every call to OnNewMail() adds a nonzero distance even if the robot is stationary, because you diff against whatever the previous pose snapshot was without ever advancing the snapshot.
Publishing in OnNewMail() is wrong by design. OnNewMail() is an interrupt-style handler — its job is to ingest and cache incoming data, nothing more. Side effects (computation, publishing) belong in Iterate().
The Correct Pattern

OnNewMail() — cache only, no computation:

cpp
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  for (auto& msg : NewMail) {
    if (msg.GetKey() == "NAV_X")
      m_x = msg.GetDouble();
    else if (msg.GetKey() == "NAV_Y")
      m_y = msg.GetDouble();
  }
  return true;
}

Iterate() — compute and publish on a stable tick:

cpp
bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  if (!m_initialized) {
    // Snapshot the starting pose; don't accumulate on first tick
    m_prev_x = m_x;
    m_prev_y = m_y;
    m_initialized = true;
    return true;
  }

  double dx = m_x - m_prev_x;
  double dy = m_y - m_prev_y;
  m_total += hypot(dx, dy);

  m_prev_x = m_x;   // advance the snapshot
  m_prev_y = m_y;

  Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total);
  return true;
}
The Mental Model
	OnNewMail()	Iterate()
Trigger	Whenever mail arrives (async)	Fixed AppTick rate (e.g. 10 Hz)
Job	Cache raw values into member vars	Use cached values to compute & publish
State mutation	Update m_x, m_y, etc.	Update m_total, m_prev_x/y, etc.
Publish	Never	Yes

Think of OnNewMail() as writing sensor readings into registers, and Iterate() as the clock edge that actually does the work. This decoupling means your computation always runs on a consistent, complete world-state snapshot — regardless of how mail happens to be batched or ordered.

The m_initialized guard is also essential: without it, your first Iterate() call diffs against zero-initialized m_prev_x/y, giving you a spurious jump in ODOM_DIST at startup.

gemini.text:
The reason your current code feels fragile is that it breaks a core architectural rule of MOOS (Mission Oriented Operating Suite) applications. You are mixing asynchronous data ingestion with synchronous state updates and publication.

Here is why your current implementation will fail or give incorrect readings in the real world, followed by how to fix it.

The Flaws in the Current Pattern

The Order/Timing Bug: OnNewMail() processes messages sequentially. If NAV_X and NAV_Y both arrive in the same mail queue, your code calculates hypot() inside the loop. If NAV_X updates first, it calculates the distance using the new X but the old Y.

Missing Initial State: On the very first loop iteration, m_prev_x and m_prev_y are likely initialized to zero. If your robot spawns at coordinates (100,500), your very first calculation will falsely register a massive spike in odometer distance.

Double Counting / Stale Data: If only NAV_X arrives in a specific mail delivery but NAV_Y hasn't changed, you will still run the distance calculation using the old m_y, potentially adding noise to your odometer.

Division of Labor: OnNewMail() vs. Iterate()

To make your MOOS app robust, you must strictly separate Inbox Management from Application Logic.

1. What belongs in OnNewMail()?

Think of OnNewMail() strictly as a mail sorter. Its only job is to unpack messages from the MOOS DB and store them into your class member variables.

Do: Update state variables (m_x = msg.GetDouble()).

Do: Set flags indicating new data has arrived if needed (e.g., m_nav_x_updated = true).

Don't: Perform physics/mathematical calculations.

Don't: Publish new data to the DB via Notify().

2. What belongs in Iterate()?

Iterate() is your application's heartbeat, controlled by the AppTick configuration in your .moos file. This is where your actual "thinking" happens.

Do: Check if you have received enough valid data to perform a calculation.

Do: Compute the actual odometry math.

Do: Update history variables (m_prev_x = m_x).

Do: Publish the results back to the MOOS community using Notify().

The Corrected Pattern

Here is how you should restructure your MOOS app class to make it bulletproof.

The Header (Odometry.h snippet)
C++
private:
  // State variables
  double m_x;
  double m_y;
  double m_prev_x;
  double m_prev_y;
  double m_total_dist;

  // Control flags
  bool m_nav_x_received;
  bool m_nav_y_received;
  bool m_first_reading; 

The Implementation (Odometry.cpp)
C++
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnNewMail(NewMail); // Good practice if using AppCasting

  for(auto &msg : NewMail) {
    std::string key = msg.GetKey();

    if(key == "NAV_X") {
      m_x = msg.GetDouble();
      m_nav_x_received = true;
    }
    else if(key == "NAV_Y") {
      m_y = msg.GetDouble();
      m_nav_y_received = true;
    }
  }
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::Iterate(); // Good practice

  // 1. Only calculate if we have received a matching pair of coordinates
  if(m_nav_x_received && m_nav_y_received) {

    // 2. Handle the initialization edge-case cleanly
    if(m_first_reading) {
      m_prev_x = m_x;
      m_prev_y = m_y;
      m_first_reading = false;
    }

    // 3. Compute the delta
    double distance = hypot(m_x - m_prev_x, m_y - m_prev_y);
    m_total_dist += distance;

    // 4. Publish the results
    Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total_dist);

    // 5. Update history and reset flags for the next Iterate cycle
    m_prev_x = m_x;
    m_prev_y = m_y;
    m_nav_x_received = false;
    m_nav_y_received = false;
  }

  return true;
}

Why this is robust:

Atomic Updates: Distance is only calculated once per Iterate() cycle, ensuring m_x and m_y are synchronized.

Spike Prevention: By checking m_first_reading, the app gracefully seeds the starting position without calculating a massive ghost leap from the origin (0,0).

Rate Decoupling: Even if your NAV data arrives at 100Hz and your AppTick is set to 10Hz, your app will seamlessly calculate the distance traveled over that 0.1-second chunk without clogging the MOOS mail system.

